<section class="panel" aria-label="2021 Grade 3 Unit 5 Lesson 11 independent equivalent-fractions tutorial"><h2>Generate Equivalent Fractions</h2><p>Original Number Talk: 2×8=16,6×8=48,10×8=80,12×8=96.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original 2021 figure</th><th>Source shaded fraction</th><th>Another valid fraction name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>2/4</td><td>4/8</td><td>Original two of four shaded, Jada re-partitions to eighths</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>1/3</td><td>2/6</td><td>One of three equal parts shaded</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>6/8</td><td>3/4</td><td>Six of eight shaded</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>6/6</td><td>2/2</td><td>One whole completely shaded</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>2/4</td><td>1/2</td><td>Original teacher-synthesis half diagram</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6</th><td>4/6</td><td>2/3</td><td>Four sixths of one whole</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7</th><td>2/8</td><td>1/4</td><td>Two eighths of one whole</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">8</th><td>4/4</td><td>2/2</td><td>All quarters shaded equals one</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">9</th><td>3/2</td><td>6/4</td><td>TWO WHOLES, each divided into halves; three of four parts across two separate wholes shaded</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Jada's 4/8 is correct for the original two shaded fourths, if each equal fourth is subdivided equally. The source also explicitly requires 1/3=2/6,6/8=3/4,6/6=2/2.</p><h3>Two-whole source diagram</h3>
<p>The final image has <strong>two separate whole rectangles</strong> and <strong>three shaded halves</strong>. It represents 3/2=6/4, not 3/4. Teachers review accurate drawn shading and source whole size.</p>
